quick q before i answer - ive seen "MEV" mentiond everywhere when ppl talk about mempools, whats that actualy
@@ reply
MEV is maximal extractable value (originally "miner extractable value"): the profit a block producer can make, beyond fees and the block reward, by choosing which transactions go into its block and in what order. Pending transactions are visible in the mempool, so a producer, or a trader who pays the producer for placement, can put its own transactions around someone else's. The standard example is a sandwich: buy just before a large visible trade moves a price, and sell just after it. All of it follows from one fact: the producer alone decides the order inside its block.

Fee ordering is the simplest use of that same power, which is where the problem was.

<solution>The three highest fees: 9, 7 and 5. Total 9 + 7 + 5 = 21 units.</solution>

<ask type="problem">Back to the block with room for 3: from the fees 4, 9, 2, 7 and 5, which three does a producer after the most revenue take, and what do they add up to?</ask>
@@ verify
fees = [4, 9, 2, 7, 5]
best = sorted(fees, reverse=True)[:3]
assert best == [9, 7, 5] and sum(best) == 21
assert '21' in solution(reply) and '21' not in visible(reply)
# the pending question is put again in new words, not repeated
import re
ask = re.search(r'<ask[^>]*>(.*?)</ask>', reply, flags=re.S).group(1)
old = re.search(r'<ask[^>]*>(.*?)</ask>', tutor[0], flags=re.S).group(1)
assert ask != old and all(str(f) in ask for f in fees)

@@@@ tangent-majority-cannot-steal
situation: tangent
topic: Double spending
options: {"strategy":"dialogue"}
voice: wordy
level: medium
expect: {"must":["signature","private key"],"mustNot":["can take (your|the) coins"],"askTypes":["short","explain"],"words":{"max":170}}
noverify: a conceptual answer; nothing is computed
@@ tutor
Suppose you run a shop that accepts a proof-of-work coin. A customer pays, you see the transaction in the newest block, and you hand over the goods straight away.

<ask type="explain">What could the customer still do to end up with both the goods and the coins, and what would it take?</ask>
@@ learner question
Is this the 51% attack thing I keep reading about? Because what I don't get is, if someone has 51% of the hash power, does that mean they basically control the chain and can just move the coins out of my wallet into theirs? That sounds like the whole thing would be broken.
@@ reply
It is related, and no: a majority of the hash power does not let anyone move your coins. Spending an output needs a valid signature from its private key, and every node rejects a block that contains a transaction with an invalid signature, however much work is behind that block. Hash power buys the ability to out-build the honest chain, which means choosing which valid transactions are included, leaving some out, and replacing recent blocks with different ones. So a majority attacker can censor, and can reverse payments they made themselves. They cannot forge a payment from you.

That second ability is the one your shop has to worry about.

<ask type="explain">The customer is the attacker, and their payment to you is in the newest block. Describe the alternative chain they would have to produce for nodes to abandon that block, and what it would contain in place of the payment.</ask>
